  "text": "Allophone: A variant of a phoneme that occurs in specific environments within a language.\n\nAnaphora: A word or phrase that refers back to a previously mentioned entity in the discourse.\n\nApocope: The omission of one or more sounds or letters from the end of a word.\n\nArtifact: An object made by humans, often studied in archaeology or linguistics for cultural insights.\n\nBabel: A biblical reference to the confusion of languages after the Tower of Babel.\n\nBilingualism: The ability to use two languages with comparable fluency.\n\nBilingual: A person who uses two languages.\n\nCalque: A loanword or expression borrowed from another language in a literal, word-for-word manner.\n\nCohesion: The way in which words and phrases are connected to form a coherent text.\n\nConcord: The agreement between words in a sentence in terms of number, gender, or case.\n\nConflation: The merging of two or more phonemes into one.\n\nConsonant: A speech sound that is produced with some obstruction of the airstream in the vocal tract.\n\nConsonant cluster: A sequence of consonants without a vowel between them.\n\nConsonant harmony: A phonological process where consonants in a word are restricted to a certain set of sounds.\n\nConsonant nasalization: A process where a consonant is pronounced with nasal resonance.\n\nConsonant mutation: A phonological process where a consonant changes in sound or character.\n\nConsonant shift: A gradual change in the sound of a consonant over time in a language.\n\nConsonant assimilation: A process in which a consonant becomes similar to a neighboring sound.\n\nCopula: A verb that links a subject to a predicate, such as \"be\" in English.\n\nDiachronic: Relating to change over time in a language.\n\nDiphthong: A vowel sound that is formed by the movement of the tongue from one vowel position to another within the same syllable.\n\nDiphthongization: The process by which a monophthong becomes a diphthong.\n\nDisplacement: The ability of language to refer to things not present in space or time.\n\nDolichotomy: The division of a word into two syllables, sometimes with the first syllable being short.\n\nEclipsing: A type of consonant mutation in which a masculine or neuter noun becomes a feminine noun.\n\nFricative: A consonant sound produced by a partial obstruction of the airstream, resulting in friction.\n\nGenitive: A case indicating possession or relationship.\n\nGeminant: A consonant that is pronounced with two successive articulations, such as a double consonant.\n\nGlottal stop: A sound made by closing the vocal cords briefly, as in the word \"uh-oh.\"\n\nHeteroglossia: The presence of multiple voices or perspectives in a text.\n\nHypercorrection: The use of a more \"correct\" form than is appropriate in a given context.\n\nIdiolect: The speech patterns of an individual speaker.\n\nIconicity: The similarity between the form of a word and its meaning.\n\nImperative: A grammatical mood used to give commands or make requests.\n\nInfinitive: A verb form that does not have a subject and is used as a noun.\n\nInfix: A morpheme inserted within a word.\n\nInvariable: A word that does not change form for different grammatical contexts.\n\nIntonation: The variation of pitch in speech that can convey meaning or emotion.\n\nInterjection: A word used to express an emotion or exclamation, such as \"Oh!\" or \"Wow!\"\n\nLabial: A sound made with the lips, such as /p/, /b/, or /m/.\n\nLabiodental: A sound made with the lower lip and",
